  "grammar": {
    "md": "**Questions that expect an answer.** Socrates' questions are built so that the boy can only say yes, and the Greek tells you which answer is expected:\n\n| question begins | expects | example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **οὐ** …; / **οὐκοῦν** …; | yes | οὐκοῦν εἴη ἂν τοιοῦτον χωρίον καὶ μεῖζον; |\n| **ἄλλο τι ἤ** …; | yes ('is it anything but …?') | ἄλλο τι ἢ δὶς δυοῖν γίγνεται; |\n| **μή** …; / **μῶν** …; | no | |\n| **ἆρα** …; | open | |\n\n**Answers.** Greek has no single word for 'yes': the answerer repeats the verb (γίγνεται), or says ναί, πάνυ γε, ἔγωγε ('I do'), ἔμοιγε ('it does to me'). 'No': οὔ, οὐ δῆτα 'certainly not'.\n\n**Potential optative in geometry.** εἴη ἂν 'there could be'; γένοιτ’ ἂν 'there might be'; πόσων ἂν εἴη ποδῶν; 'how many feet would it be?' (Unit 10). With εἰ + optative the condition is 'should-would' (future less vivid): εἰ εἴη αὕτη ἡ πλευρὰ δυοῖν ποδοῖν … πόσων ἂν εἴη;\n\n**The dual.** δυοῖν ποδοῖν 'of two feet' is a dual (Unit 11): genitive/dative -οιν. The genitive expresses measure: τὸ χωρίον ποδῶν τεττάρων 'an area of four feet'.\n\n**The argument.** A square of side 2 has area 4 (δὶς δύο). The double square has area 8. The boy says its side is double (4) and is wrong: that square is 16. He *thinks* he knows (οἴεται εἰδέναι) but does not know (οἶδεν οὖν; οὐ δῆτα). Socrates claims he teaches nothing (οὐδὲν διδάσκω) but only asks (ἐρωτῶ πάντα): the answer, he will say, the boy recollects (ἀναμιμνῄσκεται) from a time before his birth. (The true answer, drawn later in the dialogue: the side of the double square is the diagonal of the first.)",
    "paradigms": [
      "poieo"
    ],
    "diagram": "diagram-dual"
  },
