  "culture": {
    "title": "Plato, the Meno and the Academy",
    "md": "The **Meno** is one of Plato's shorter dialogues, written in the 380s BC, soon after he founded his school in the grove of the hero Akademos, a mile outside the Dipylon gate of Athens: the **Academy** (Ἀκαδήμεια). Its dramatic date is about 402 BC. Meno, a rich young Thessalian aristocrat visiting Athens, asks whether virtue can be taught; he later commanded Greek mercenaries for Cyrus, and Xenophon's *Anabasis* paints him as greedy and treacherous.\n\nWhen Meno objects that one cannot search for what one does not know, Socrates tells him of priests and poets who say the soul is immortal and has seen everything before birth; learning is **recollection** (ἀνάμνησις). To prove it he calls over one of Meno's household slaves, a boy who speaks Greek but has never studied geometry, and draws squares in the sand. The boy first answers wrongly (doubling the side of a square quadruples it), then is reduced to ἀπορία, then, still only by questions, finds that the square on the **diagonal** is double the first.\n\nThe scene is the earliest surviving geometrical demonstration in Greek, a generation before Euclid. In the Academy, it was said, an inscription warned: 'Let no one ignorant of geometry enter.' The site of the Academy, with the remains of a gymnasium and later buildings, is an archaeological park in modern Athens.",
    "image": "phil-culture-4-academy"
  },
